<body background="/web/20060707200620im_/http://www.mitsawokett.com/Cemeteries/Christ%27s%20Church%20near%20Harbeson/Christs%20Church%20Harbeson%20cemetery.htm/Wolfcreek2.jpg" text="#990000" vlink="#333300" alink="#996600" link="#003333">
<p align="center">&nbsp;</p><p align="center"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><img src="/web/20060707200620im_/http://www.mitsawokett.com/Cemeteries/Christ%27s%20Church%20near%20Harbeson/Christs%20Church%20Harbeson%20cemetery.htm/ind-footer.gif" alt="Footer"></font></p><p align="center">&nbsp;</p><h6 align="CENTER"><b><font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="6">MAIN 
MENU</font><font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="4"><br><br></font></b></h6><h6 align="CENTER"><b><font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="4">(<a href="MENU%20LARGE%20PRINT.htm">Click 
here for LARGE PRINT MENU)</a><br><br><br>Research assets on web sites '<a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20060707200620/http://www.mitsawokett.com/">Mitsawokett</a>' 
<br>&amp; <br>some from Lynn Jackson's '<a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20060707200620/http://www.lynncjackson.com/family/main.htm">Moors 
&amp; Nanticokes</a>'<br><br></font></b></h6><blockquote><blockquote><blockquote><blockquote><p><font face="Albertus Medium" size="-1"><b><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="-2" color="#990000">Mitsawokett</font></b><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="-2" color="#990000"> 
is intended to assist the descendants of the original people of Delaware find 
their ancestral families, whether Lenni Lenape or Nanticoke. Their allied ancestral 
African, Iberian or other European lines will be mentioned but not further persued, 
being beyond the scope of this site's research.</font></font> </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="-2" color="#990000">A 
cautionary note: Much of the information contained on these pages is hearsay information 
obtained verbally or by e-mail or letter. Some is copied from official documents. 
Researchers should be cautious about accepting secondhand information from these 
pages without checking it's accuracy against primary sources. Copying may introduce 
error; certainly oral history will, too. &nbsp;The best source is a record created 
at the time a birth, marriage or death occurs.&nbsp; But oral history, with all 
its faults, will continue to be vital to our attempt to construct the story of 
our Native American past.&nbsp;</font> </p></blockquote></blockquote></blockquote></blockquote><p align="CENTER">&nbsp;</p><td class="listing first" height="100%" bordercolor="#FFFFFF"><div align="CENTER"><table width="86%" border="0" bordercolor="#FFFFFF" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr bgcolor="#990000"><td width="276">&nbsp;</td><td width="301">&nbsp;</td><td width="274">&nbsp;</td></tr><tr bordercolor="#FFFFFF"><td width="276">&nbsp;</td><td width="277">&nbsp;</td><td width="274">&nbsp;</td></tr><tr bordercolor="#FFFFFF"><td width="276"><font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="-1"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20060707200620/http://www.mitsawokett.com/Articles&amp;Research.htm"><b>Articles 
